# Each phrase wraps a behavior in the ability to have it. When the
# subject acts, the fix gives the verb the subject (`Source lets you
# iterate over lines` becomes `Source iterates over lines`). When the
# reader acts, the fix makes the reader the subject with `you can`
# (`this feature allows you to disable the controller` becomes `with
# this feature, you can disable the controller`), because the first fix
# there claims that the feature disables it.
#
# The intent rule holds the phrases that wrap a behavior in its purpose
# instead. `is used to`, `are used to`, and `can be used to` stay out,
# because the passive rule reports them.
#
# It hit 441 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# wrapped a behavior that one of the two fixes states directly.
expr main [(allows you to), (allow you to), (allows users to), (allow users to), (enables you to), (enable you to), (enables users to), (lets you), (let you), (lets users), (makes it possible to), (make it possible to), (gives you the ability to), (provides the ability to), (provides a way to), (offers the ability to), (offers a way to), (helps to), (help to), (helps you), (help you), (is capable of), (are capable of), (has the capability to), (have the capability to), (makes it easy to), (make it easy to), (makes it easier to), (makes it simple to)]
let message "Ability phrase. When the subject acts, make the behavior its verb. When the reader acts, write `you can`."
let description "Flags phrases that wrap a behavior in the ability to have it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Source iterates over the lines of one or more documents."
allows "The key used to sign tokens lives in the secrets store."
